<p class="prose">The LNG figures for all three major North Asian importers cluster between 60 and 65 percent. Unlike the crude dependency numbers, these figures do not carry a meaningful bypass correction. The Lombok bypass exists as a routing option; it does not resolve the supply availability problem. For accumulation purposes, the LNG component of Malacca exposure should be treated as harder than the crude component.</p>

<hr class="section-rule" />

<!-- SECTION 6 -->
<div class="section-eyebrow">The accumulation implication</div>
<h2 class="article-section-title">What the correct figure does to the reserve calculation.</h2>

<p class="prose">An underwriter sizing a treaty layer on Chinese crude exposure with an 80 percent Malacca dependency assumption is applying a structural factor 43 percent higher than the CDM bilateral figure. The implied PML is wrong in the same direction. The difference in implied 14-day VaR for China crude between the two assumptions is approximately $34 billion at annual trade values. In a reinsurance treaty, that is a structural mispricing, not a modelling tolerance.</p>

<p class="prose">The CDM framework treats Malacca exposure in three distinct buckets:</p>

<div class="bucket-grid">
 <div class="bucket">
  <div class="bucket-num">Bucket 01</div>
  <h3>Genuinely captive</h3>
  <p>Cargo from origins with no viable routing alternative to Malacca: specific Southeast Asian LNG supply chains, and crude origins where the Lombok detour is not commercially or operationally viable. A Malacca disruption is a supply halt for this cargo. Full severity applies.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bucket locked">
  <div class="bucket-num">Bucket 02</div>
  <h3>Economically captive</h3>
  <p>Cargo that could reroute via Lombok at a cost that, below a threshold disruption severity or duration, makes Malacca transit preferable. Above approximately 10 days of disruption, Lombok becomes commercially rational. This cargo faces rerouting cost, not a supply halt.</p>
 </div>
 <div class="bucket locked">
  <div class="bucket-num">Bucket 03</div>
  <h3>Origin-diversified</h3>
  <p>Chinese crude from Russian and African origins that does not transit Malacca. A Malacca event does not affect this supply at all. It contributes nothing to Malacca accumulation regardless of what dependency figure is applied.</p>
 </div>
</div>

<p class="prose">The 55.8 percent figure covers Buckets 01 and 02 combined. The fraction in Bucket 01 alone, with no viable bypass, is smaller still. The 80 percent figure collapses all three buckets into a single number, implicitly treating Bucket 03 as Malacca-exposed and Bucket 02 as having no bypass. Both assumptions are wrong in the same direction, and they compound.</p>

<div class="callout-verdict">
 <div class="callout-verdict-label">The model test</div>
 <div class="callout-verdict-body">Apply 80 percent and 55.8 percent Malacca dependency to China's crude import volume and run the same closure scenario. The 14-day VaR difference is approximately <strong>$34 billion</strong>. The war risk PML difference at standard attachment rates is in the hundreds of millions. The 80 percent figure has been embedded in corridor risk models for over a decade. The bilateral dependency layer in the CDM is what corrects it, at the (importer, chokepoint, commodity) level, without the origin-mix averaging that produces the throughput figure.</div>
</div>

